A||||3||Scratchy||auto trans mysteries?||||5
Z||000000||Scratchy||11-11-1999||11:24 AM||||I'd like to know how the Fiero auto transmission work? What makes it shift? Hydraulic, electric, valves, solenoids?  What is a shift kit?  What does it do? If you could control the gear shift manually and the TC lockup in any gear, would it feel like a manual?  For example, you get into first, accelerate a little (the TC slip, so no stall), then it a switch to lock the TC (no more slip, like a manual, near 100 percent efficiency), then let of the gas pedal, hit the switch again to unlock the TC and move to second gear, accelerate a little etc...To make it clear...would it be possible to lock up the TC in any gear to achieve better efficiency?  Am I in total darkness?<P>Thanks for any help,<BR>Fred||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-11-1999||12:05 PM||||You'll find GM did a rather good job of building the TH125c. There is very little you can do to them to make them better or more efficient.<P>TH125c uses a throtle position cable to determin much of it's shift timing. The rest is internal. There is no vacuum modulator. TC lockup will happen when 3 conditions are met. 1. foot off brake. 2. ECM sees vehicle speed constant above 35mph. 3. trans is in 3rd gear. TC lockup is electric/hydraulic.<P>A shift kit changes the shift timing to provide quicker shifting between gears.<P>It is posible to convert most automatics to manual operation. This is done with a variation of the shift kit. I do not recomend this be done to street driven cars.<P>You could make the tranny so the TC will lock in any gear. Odds are you'll end up breaking the tranny if you do this. More importantly you will actually lose performance doing it. The TC provides a torque multiplier to the setup. You actually get more torque from a working TC than a locked clutch.<P>If you want better performance from a TH125c look for ones with a lower final gear set (diferential) in them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Scratchy||11-11-1999||12:13 PM||||Wow, that was quick and very informative, thanks.<P>Another question then:  Is there a shift kit for the fiero (which company...any experience with it...) and what is the modification required for the manual operation as you stated?  And how can I change the final drive ratio (where to find the gears needed and which ones, is it from a manual?)?<P>Thanks again,<BR>Fred||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||11-11-1999||12:42 PM||||You can try <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>tranmission exchange co.</A> they carry the transgo kit. I haven't used a shift kit in the 125. I don't find they really need it.<P>I don't know if anyone makes a manual conversion.<P>Your best bets are find the ID codes for the TH125, no i don't have them, that tell you exactly what modle the one you have is. You'll likely need the GM factory books or their equivalent to get these numbers. You can then look for ones in salvage yards with the gears you want. Changing finals is a critical job best left to a tranny shop. It requires very presice setup or you break the gears. You may be able to get gears as parts from transmission exchange co.||||reg||1
